{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"inarray-constant-list-set-oracle-review","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inArray"},{"type":"text","value":" constant-list Set oracle review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed source head: the commit that adds this record on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"perf-in-set"},{"type":"text","value":". The\nproduction change is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"18ea072b5"},{"type":"text","value":". This record adds the audit only."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"contract-and-evidence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract and evidence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inArray(value, list)"},{"type":"text","value":" is SQL "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"IN"},{"type":"text","value":" under three-valued logic. A null or\nundefined value is UNKNOWN ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":"). A list that is not an array is FALSE. The\nresult is TRUE when some non-null list item equals the value under the\nevaluator's equality, and FALSE otherwise."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The authority is the evaluator contract in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/src/query/compiler/evaluators.ts"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"valuesEqual"},{"type":"text","value":" treats "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":" and\nan invalid Date as equal to each other and to nothing else.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalizeEqualityOperand"},{"type":"text","value":" compares a valid Date as its millisecond timestamp,\ncompares byte arrays by content, and compares Temporal values by kind and\nstring. Every other pair compares with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"==="},{"type":"text","value":". The existing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"in"},{"type":"text","value":" tests in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"evaluators-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and the WHERE publication oracle state the same\nrules for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"eq"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The change keeps this result and changes its cost. Join demand\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/src/query/live/subset-demand-controller.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSegment"},{"type":"text","value":")\nsends one constant "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inArray"},{"type":"text","value":" list. Before the change, each row was compared\nwith every item, so a cold join over 10,000 rows with no source index took\nabout 1.1 s. Now a constant list becomes a Set of normalized keys. Byte arrays\nstay out of the Set and keep their content comparison."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle is\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/query/compiler/in-evaluator-oracle.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". Its\nmodel, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"referenceIn"},{"type":"text","value":", walks the list and applies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"referenceEqual"},{"type":"text","value":", which\nrestates the rules above without importing any production comparison or\nnormalization helper."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"findings-during-the-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Findings during the review"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Buffer from another realm."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The first version of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"referenceEqual"},{"type":"text","value":"\nchecked bytes with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"instanceof Uint8Array"},{"type":"text","value":". Under jsdom, Node's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Buffer"},{"type":"text","value":" comes\nfrom another realm, so that check returned false for a Buffer, and the\nreference called a Buffer and an equal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Uint8Array"},{"type":"text","value":" unequal. Production was\ncorrect. The reference now checks "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ArrayBuffer.isView"},{"type":"text","value":" and the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[object Uint8Array]"},{"type":"text","value":" tag."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The byte-array work law."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The first version of the fix put every list item\nin the Set through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalizeValue"},{"type":"text","value":", and looked up each row value the same\nway. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalizeValue"},{"type":"text","value":" encodes a byte array as a string, one character per\nbyte. The existing law in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/query/compiler/binary-equality-work.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compares <n> bytes with 'in'/... without encoding strings"},{"type":"text","value":") counts calls to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"String.fromCharCode"},{"type":"text","value":" during evaluation. All 24 of its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"in"},{"type":"text","value":" cases with 129\nor 65,536 bytes failed, and so did "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compares a MiB using in without caching mutable bytes"},{"type":"text","value":". The\nfixed version compares a byte value only with the byte items in the list, by\ncontent, and does not encode it."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"mutant-results","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutant results"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The production source was restored after each run."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutant"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Set holds raw items, not normalized keys"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assertion failure: 4 of 11 tests."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The row value is not normalized before the lookup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assertion failure: 2 of 11 tests."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A byte value compares with byte items by reference"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assertion failure: 3 of 11 tests."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A byte value is looked up in the Set"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assertion failure: 3 of 11 tests."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Byte items are also put in the Set"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Equivalent within the tested domain. A byte value never reaches the Set, so no result changes. The only difference is encoding at compile time, which the work law does not count: it passed 76 of 76."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(First fix) every value goes through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalizeValue"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Caught by the byte-array work law: 25 failures. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"in"},{"type":"text","value":" oracle passed, because the results were correct."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-012-requirement-audit","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 requirement audit"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable. The law and its authority are above. The claim is limited to the boolean result of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"in"},{"type":"text","value":". WHERE publication, indexes and subset loading have other owners, which the oracle prose names."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"referenceIn"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"referenceEqual"},{"type":"text","value":" are a linear walk over the stated rules. They do not import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"valuesEqual"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalizeValue"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isUint8Array"},{"type":"text","value":" or any other production helper."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable. The opening prose states the contract, the model, the value domain, the production driver, the checkpoint and the limits."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable. The domain is a fixed pool of 25 values that holds the boundary of each rule. Lists have zero to six items and may repeat items. The list comes from a constant or from a row field. Pinned cases reconstruct each boundary: a Date and its timestamp, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":" and an invalid Date, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":", a BigInt and a Number, equal bytes, bytes and their internal key, a structural copy, and an empty list. The calibration test removes normalization, and the checker rejects it."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable. The driver compiles the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"in"},{"type":"text","value":" IR with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compileExpression"},{"type":"text","value":" and evaluates it per row, as a WHERE clause does. It evaluates one other row first, so a cached list that should not be reused is also checked."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable. The calibration test and the mutants above reach the comparison and fail with an assertion. Each outcome is classified in the table."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable. The same property and budget run twice: once with fixed seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0x2029"},{"type":"text","value":", and once with an unseeded or replay seed through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"oraclePropertyOptions"},{"type":"text","value":". The property is registered as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"evaluators.in"},{"type":"text","value":", so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_SEED"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PATH"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PROPERTY"},{"type":"text","value":" replay a reported failure directly. The property does not use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fc.commands"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inapplicable. The model is a stateless function of the value and the list."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable. The terms match production: \"UNKNOWN\" is the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" result, and \"normalized key\" is the value that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalizeValue"},{"type":"text","value":" returns."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable. The check throws one error that names the expected and actual results and the case. It has no cleanup step, so no cleanup error can replace it. fast-check reports the shrunk counterexample with its seed and path."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applicable and satisfied. The byte-array work law is a second, different formulation. It measures the work of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"in"},{"type":"text","value":" and not its result, and it caught the first fix that the result oracle accepted."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inapplicable. The oracle protects no threshold or range law."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inapplicable. No controlled provider supplies a premise."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"performance-evidence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Performance evidence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repo benchmark ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scripts/bench/incremental-update.ts"},{"type":"text","value":") ran three\ninterleaved times on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" and on the branch. These are median cold hydrate\ntimes with no source index:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query, 10,000 rows"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Branch"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list + author"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1,101–1,221 ms"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"79–87 ms"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list + comment count"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1,024–1,102 ms"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"99–112 ms"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"list + 3 recent comments"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1,004–1,019 ms"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"91–94 ms"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Median write times did not change beyond noise. Two of the three comparisons\nwere 1.00× and 0.98×, and a same-code comparison was 0.96×."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"inarray-constant-list-set-oracle-review","text":"inArray constant-list Set oracle review","level":1},{"id":"contract-and-evidence","text":"Contract and evidence","level":2},{"id":"findings-during-the-review","text":"Findings during the review","level":2},{"id":"mutant-results","text":"Mutant results","level":2},{"id":"orc-012-requirement-audit","text":"ORC-012 requirement audit","level":2},{"id":"performance-evidence","text":"Performance evidence","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# `inArray` constant-list Set oracle review\n\nReviewed source head: the commit that adds this record on `perf-in-set`. The\nproduction change is `18ea072b5`. This record adds the audit only.\n\n## Contract and evidence\n\n`inArray(value, list)` is SQL `IN` under three-valued logic. A null or\nundefined value is UNKNOWN (`null`). A list that is not an array is FALSE. The\nresult is TRUE when some non-null list item equals the value under the\nevaluator's equality, and FALSE otherwise.\n\nThe authority is the evaluator contract in\n`packages/db/src/query/compiler/evaluators.ts`: `valuesEqual` treats `NaN` and\nan invalid Date as equal to each other and to nothing else.\n`normalizeEqualityOperand` compares a valid Date as its millisecond timestamp,\ncompares byte arrays by content, and compares Temporal values by kind and\nstring. Every other pair compares with `===`. The existing `in` tests in\n`evaluators-oracle.test.ts` and the WHERE publication oracle state the same\nrules for `eq`.\n\nThe change keeps this result and changes its cost. Join demand\n(`packages/db/src/query/live/subset-demand-controller.ts`, `createSegment`)\nsends one constant `inArray` list. Before the change, each row was compared\nwith every item, so a cold join over 10,000 rows with no source index took\nabout 1.1 s. Now a constant list becomes a Set of normalized keys. Byte arrays\nstay out of the Set and keep their content comparison.\n\nThe oracle is\n`packages/db/tests/query/compiler/in-evaluator-oracle.property.test.ts`. Its\nmodel, `referenceIn`, walks the list and applies `referenceEqual`, which\nrestates the rules above without importing any production comparison or\nnormalization helper.\n\n## Findings during the review\n\n- **A Buffer from another realm.** The first version of `referenceEqual`\n  checked bytes with `instanceof Uint8Array`. Under jsdom, Node's `Buffer` comes\n  from another realm, so that check returned false for a Buffer, and the\n  reference called a Buffer and an equal `Uint8Array` unequal. Production was\n  correct. The reference now checks `ArrayBuffer.isView` and the\n  `[object Uint8Array]` tag.\n- **The byte-array work law.** The first version of the fix put every list item\n  in the Set through `normalizeValue`, and looked up each row value the same\n  way. `normalizeValue` encodes a byte array as a string, one character per\n  byte. The existing law in\n  `packages/db/tests/query/compiler/binary-equality-work.test.ts` (`compares\n  <n> bytes with 'in'/... without encoding strings`) counts calls to\n  `String.fromCharCode` during evaluation. All 24 of its `in` cases with 129\n  or 65,536 bytes failed, and so did `compares a MiB using in without caching\n  mutable bytes`. The\n  fixed version compares a byte value only with the byte items in the list, by\n  content, and does not encode it.\n\n## Mutant results\n\nThe production source was restored after each run.\n\n| Mutant | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| The Set holds raw items, not normalized keys | Assertion failure: 4 of 11 tests. |\n| The row value is not normalized before the lookup | Assertion failure: 2 of 11 tests. |\n| A byte value compares with byte items by reference | Assertion failure: 3 of 11 tests. |\n| A byte value is looked up in the Set | Assertion failure: 3 of 11 tests. |\n| Byte items are also put in the Set | Equivalent within the tested domain. A byte value never reaches the Set, so no result changes. The only difference is encoding at compile time, which the work law does not count: it passed 76 of 76. |\n| (First fix) every value goes through `normalizeValue` | Caught by the byte-array work law: 25 failures. The `in` oracle passed, because the results were correct. |\n\n## ORC-012 requirement audit\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Applicable. The law and its authority are above. The claim is limited to the boolean result of `in`. WHERE publication, indexes and subset loading have other owners, which the oracle prose names. |\n| ORC-002 | Applicable. `referenceIn` and `referenceEqual` are a linear walk over the stated rules. They do not import `valuesEqual`, `normalizeValue`, `isUint8Array` or any other production helper. |\n| ORC-003 | Applicable. The opening prose states the contract, the model, the value domain, the production driver, the checkpoint and the limits. |\n| ORC-004 | Applicable. The domain is a fixed pool of 25 values that holds the boundary of each rule. Lists have zero to six items and may repeat items. The list comes from a constant or from a row field. Pinned cases reconstruct each boundary: a Date and its timestamp, `NaN` and an invalid Date, `-0` and `0`, a BigInt and a Number, equal bytes, bytes and their internal key, a structural copy, and an empty list. The calibration test removes normalization, and the checker rejects it. |\n| ORC-005 | Applicable. The driver compiles the public `in` IR with `compileExpression` and evaluates it per row, as a WHERE clause does. It evaluates one other row first, so a cached list that should not be reused is also checked. |\n| ORC-006 | Applicable. The calibration test and the mutants above reach the comparison and fail with an assertion. Each outcome is classified in the table. |\n| ORC-007 | Applicable. The same property and budget run twice: once with fixed seed `0x2029`, and once with an unseeded or replay seed through `oraclePropertyOptions`. The property is registered as `evaluators.in`, so `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_SEED`, `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PATH` and `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PROPERTY` replay a reported failure directly. The property does not use `fc.commands`. |\n| ORC-008 | Inapplicable. The model is a stateless function of the value and the list. |\n| ORC-009 | Applicable. The terms match production: \"UNKNOWN\" is the `null` result, and \"normalized key\" is the value that `normalizeValue` returns. |\n| ORC-010 | Applicable. The check throws one error that names the expected and actual results and the case. It has no cleanup step, so no cleanup error can replace it. fast-check reports the shrunk counterexample with its seed and path. |\n| ORC-011 | Applicable and satisfied. The byte-array work law is a second, different formulation. It measures the work of `in` and not its result, and it caught the first fix that the result oracle accepted. |\n| ORC-013 | Inapplicable. The oracle protects no threshold or range law. |\n| ORC-014 | Inapplicable. No controlled provider supplies a premise. |\n\n## Performance evidence\n\nThe repo benchmark (`scripts/bench/incremental-update.ts`) ran three\ninterleaved times on `main` and on the branch. These are median cold hydrate\ntimes with no source index:\n\n| Query, 10,000 rows | `main` | Branch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| list + author | 1,101–1,221 ms | 79–87 ms |\n| list + comment count | 1,024–1,102 ms | 99–112 ms |\n| list + 3 recent comments | 1,004–1,019 ms | 91–94 ms |\n\nMedian write times did not change beyond noise. Two of the three comparisons\nwere 1.00× and 0.98×, and a same-code comparison was 0.96×.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# `inArray` constant-list Set oracle review\n\nReviewed source head: the commit that adds this record on `perf-in-set`. The\nproduction change is `18ea072b5`. This record adds the audit only.\n\n## Contract and evidence\n\n`inArray(value, list)` is SQL `IN` under three-valued logic. A null or\nundefined value is UNKNOWN (`null`). A list that is not an array is FALSE. The\nresult is TRUE when some non-null list item equals the value under the\nevaluator's equality, and FALSE otherwise.\n\nThe authority is the evaluator contract in\n`packages/db/src/query/compiler/evaluators.ts`: `valuesEqual` treats `NaN` and\nan invalid Date as equal to each other and to nothing else.\n`normalizeEqualityOperand` compares a valid Date as its millisecond timestamp,\ncompares byte arrays by content, and compares Temporal values by kind and\nstring. Every other pair compares with `===`. The existing `in` tests in\n`evaluators-oracle.test.ts` and the WHERE publication oracle state the same\nrules for `eq`.\n\nThe change keeps this result and changes its cost. Join demand\n(`packages/db/src/query/live/subset-demand-controller.ts`, `createSegment`)\nsends one constant `inArray` list. Before the change, each row was compared\nwith every item, so a cold join over 10,000 rows with no source index took\nabout 1.1 s. Now a constant list becomes a Set of normalized keys. Byte arrays\nstay out of the Set and keep their content comparison.\n\nThe oracle is\n`packages/db/tests/query/compiler/in-evaluator-oracle.property.test.ts`. Its\nmodel, `referenceIn`, walks the list and applies `referenceEqual`, which\nrestates the rules above without importing any production comparison or\nnormalization helper.\n\n## Findings during the review\n\n- **A Buffer from another realm.** The first version of `referenceEqual`\n  checked bytes with `instanceof Uint8Array`. Under jsdom, Node's `Buffer` comes\n  from another realm, so that check returned false for a Buffer, and the\n  reference called a Buffer and an equal `Uint8Array` unequal. Production was\n  correct. The reference now checks `ArrayBuffer.isView` and the\n  `[object Uint8Array]` tag.\n- **The byte-array work law.** The first version of the fix put every list item\n  in the Set through `normalizeValue`, and looked up each row value the same\n  way. `normalizeValue` encodes a byte array as a string, one character per\n  byte. The existing law in\n  `packages/db/tests/query/compiler/binary-equality-work.test.ts` (`compares\n  <n> bytes with 'in'/... without encoding strings`) counts calls to\n  `String.fromCharCode` during evaluation. All 24 of its `in` cases with 129\n  or 65,536 bytes failed, and so did `compares a MiB using in without caching\n  mutable bytes`. The\n  fixed version compares a byte value only with the byte items in the list, by\n  content, and does not encode it.\n\n## Mutant results\n\nThe production source was restored after each run.\n\n| Mutant | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| The Set holds raw items, not normalized keys | Assertion failure: 4 of 11 tests. |\n| The row value is not normalized before the lookup | Assertion failure: 2 of 11 tests. |\n| A byte value compares with byte items by reference | Assertion failure: 3 of 11 tests. |\n| A byte value is looked up in the Set | Assertion failure: 3 of 11 tests. |\n| Byte items are also put in the Set | Equivalent within the tested domain. A byte value never reaches the Set, so no result changes. The only difference is encoding at compile time, which the work law does not count: it passed 76 of 76. |\n| (First fix) every value goes through `normalizeValue` | Caught by the byte-array work law: 25 failures. The `in` oracle passed, because the results were correct. |\n\n## ORC-012 requirement audit\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Applicable. The law and its authority are above. The claim is limited to the boolean result of `in`. WHERE publication, indexes and subset loading have other owners, which the oracle prose names. |\n| ORC-002 | Applicable. `referenceIn` and `referenceEqual` are a linear walk over the stated rules. They do not import `valuesEqual`, `normalizeValue`, `isUint8Array` or any other production helper. |\n| ORC-003 | Applicable. The opening prose states the contract, the model, the value domain, the production driver, the checkpoint and the limits. |\n| ORC-004 | Applicable. The domain is a fixed pool of 25 values that holds the boundary of each rule. Lists have zero to six items and may repeat items. The list comes from a constant or from a row field. Pinned cases reconstruct each boundary: a Date and its timestamp, `NaN` and an invalid Date, `-0` and `0`, a BigInt and a Number, equal bytes, bytes and their internal key, a structural copy, and an empty list. The calibration test removes normalization, and the checker rejects it. |\n| ORC-005 | Applicable. The driver compiles the public `in` IR with `compileExpression` and evaluates it per row, as a WHERE clause does. It evaluates one other row first, so a cached list that should not be reused is also checked. |\n| ORC-006 | Applicable. The calibration test and the mutants above reach the comparison and fail with an assertion. Each outcome is classified in the table. |\n| ORC-007 | Applicable. The same property and budget run twice: once with fixed seed `0x2029`, and once with an unseeded or replay seed through `oraclePropertyOptions`. The property is registered as `evaluators.in`, so `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_SEED`, `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PATH` and `TANSTACK_DB_ORACLE_PROPERTY` replay a reported failure directly. The property does not use `fc.commands`. |\n| ORC-008 | Inapplicable. The model is a stateless function of the value and the list. |\n| ORC-009 | Applicable. The terms match production: \"UNKNOWN\" is the `null` result, and \"normalized key\" is the value that `normalizeValue` returns. |\n| ORC-010 | Applicable. The check throws one error that names the expected and actual results and the case. It has no cleanup step, so no cleanup error can replace it. fast-check reports the shrunk counterexample with its seed and path. |\n| ORC-011 | Applicable and satisfied. The byte-array work law is a second, different formulation. It measures the work of `in` and not its result, and it caught the first fix that the result oracle accepted. |\n| ORC-013 | Inapplicable. The oracle protects no threshold or range law. |\n| ORC-014 | Inapplicable. No controlled provider supplies a premise. |\n\n## Performance evidence\n\nThe repo benchmark (`scripts/bench/incremental-update.ts`) ran three\ninterleaved times on `main` and on the branch. These are median cold hydrate\ntimes with no source index:\n\n| Query, 10,000 rows | `main` | Branch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| list + author | 1,101–1,221 ms | 79–87 ms |\n| list + comment count | 1,024–1,102 ms | 99–112 ms |\n| list + 3 recent comments | 1,004–1,019 ms | 91–94 ms |\n\nMedian write times did not change beyond noise. Two of the three comparisons\nwere 1.00× and 0.98×, and a same-code comparison was 0.96×.\n"}
